From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 08:51:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Remember what a position answered the pathfinder's vertical
 offset scan

findPathOptions asks for the vertical offset of the node above the one it is
expanding and then of each of its four neighbours, and getSafePoint asks again
for the block below a candidate it wants to step down onto. A node that four
neighbours border is therefore scanned from all four of them, and every scan
walks the mob's whole footprint - twelve blocks for an iron golem, each one a
block read through the chunk.

The answer depends only on the position, the block access, the mob's size and
the three flags, and all of those are fixed for the length of one search:
initProcessor installs them, and the only one that moves afterwards -
avoidsWater, cleared by getPathPointTo for a swimming mob - moves before the
first offset is asked for. So a generation counter bumped once per search
invalidates the whole table without touching it.

The table is shared per thread rather than held per navigator: there is one
PathfinderNormal per mob, and a table large enough to be worth having would be
tens of kilobytes each.

The static PathfinderNormal.a keeps no memo - its other caller passes its own
world and flags.

What can go wrong with a cache of a pure function is invalidation, not
bookkeeping, so the audit under debugVerifyAthenaIndexes rescans on every hit
and compares rather than rebuilding anything.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

